PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
I currently favor D: CAGGACCGGTTTCAGATGCG(CGG). It is a full 20-nt spCas9 protospacer with canonical CGG PAM, maps to the second exon on the reverse strand, and its reverse complement (CGCATCTGAAACCGGTCCTG) matches the exon-2 sequence in the context CCGCGCATCTGAAACCGGTCCTGTGG. It avoids the obvious intronic low-complexity/repeat regions and has no apparent low-complexity character.

Colleagues 2 and 3 independently selected D for these reasons. Colleague 1 selected E, but as written E appears to be only 19 nt: GCATCTGAAACCGGTCCTG(TGG), whereas the true 20-nt plus-strand target in that locus would be CGCATCTGAAACCGGTCCTG(TGG). Thus E may be a typo or too short. Colleague 4 selected K, but K also appears to be only 19 nt as written: CTGCTCTACCCAATCGCCA(TGG); the corrected full exon-2 target may be ACTGCTCTACCCAATCGCCA(TGG), but that is not exactly the listed choice.

Other choices are less suitable: N is in exon 1; H and J are in the intronic repetitive/low-complexity region; J has invalid PAM TTT; O has invalid PAM ACC and is too short; L is too short; C maps to exon 1 by reverse complement. Valid NGG PAM choices in exon 2 appear to include D, and possibly E/K if corrected, but D is the only clearly full-length valid exon-2 target as written. Therefore my current best answer remains D, with note that E and K may be length/typo concerns.
}

